# Website/Script-Based Access Denial Script

Below are several approaches to deny access to a website or service using different methods:

## 1. Basic HTML Access Denial Page

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Access Denied</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            padding: 50px;
            color: #333;
        }
        h1 {
            color: #d9534f;
        }
        .container {
            max-width: 600px;
            margin: 0 auto;
        }
    </style>
    <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="5;url=/">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Access Denied</h1>
        <p>You do not have permission to access this page or service.</p>
        <p>Please contact the administrator if you believe this is an error.</p>
        <p>Redirecting to home page in 5 seconds...</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

## 2. Node.js Express Middleware for Access Control

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Middleware to check access
function checkAccess(req, res, next) {
    const allowedIPs = ['192.168.1.100', '10.0.0.2'];
    const clientIP = req.ip || req.connection.remoteAddress;

    if (allowedIPs.includes(clientIP)) {
        next(); // Allow access
    } else {
        res.status(403).send('Access Denied: Your IP is not whitelisted');
    }
}

// Apply middleware to all routes
app.use(checkAccess);

// Your routes
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
    res.send('Welcome to the restricted site');
});

const PORT = 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

## 3. PHP Access Control Script

```php
<?php
// Check for allowed IPs
$allowed_ips = array('192.168.1.100', '10.0.0.2');
$client_ip = $_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR'];

// Check for authentication
$is_authenticated = isset($_SESSION['authenticated']) && $_SESSION['authenticated'];

// Deny access if conditions aren't met
if (!in_array($client_ip, $allowed_ips) && !$is_authenticated) {
    header('HTTP/1.0 403 Forbidden');
    die('
        <h1>403 Access Denied</h1>
        <p>You don\'t have permission to access this resource.</p>
    ');
}

// Continue with your website code if access is granted
?>
```

## 4. .htaccess Deny Rules (Apache)

```apache
# Block by IP
Deny from 192.168.1.5
Deny from 203.0.113.10

# Block by user agent
RewriteEngine On
RewriteCond %{HTTP_USER_AGENT} ^BadBot [NC]
RewriteRule ^.* - [F,L]

# Password protect directory
AuthType Basic
AuthName "Restricted Area"
AuthUserFile /path/to/.htpasswd
Require valid-user
```

## 5. Nginx Access Control

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    location / {
        # Block specific IPs
        deny 192.168.1.5;
        deny 203.0.113.10;

        # Allow only specific IPs
        allow 192.168.1.100;
        allow 10.0.0.2;
        deny all;

        # Other configurations...
    }

    # Block certain user agents
    if ($http_user_agent ~* (wget|curl|BadBot)) {
        return 403;
    }
}
```

## Important Notes:

1. **Legal Considerations**: Only implement access control for resources you own or administer.

2. **Security**: The IP-based methods can be bypassed if users change their IP (via VPN or proxy).

3. **Better Alternatives**: For robust access control, consider:
   - Authentication systems (OAuth, JWT, etc.)
   - Web Application Firewalls (WAF)
   - Cloud-based access control solutions

4. **Testing**: Always test these in a development environment before deploying to production.

